# Working with Inputs

Input elements have a notion of **status** that we can read and modify from JavaScript. For a text input, the status is the text in it. For a checkbox, it is whether the box is checked. For radio buttons, it is which option is selected.

Here are the properties and methods we will use, on an element `el` fetched with `querySelector`:

- **`el.value`** is the current value of the input.
- **`el.focus()`** gives the element keyboard focus, so the next key the user presses goes into it.
- **`el.select()`** selects all the contents of the input.
- **`el.disabled`** controls whether the input is disabled.
- **`el.checked`** is whether a checkbox or radio button is on.

## Text Inputs and `.value`

A text input is an `<input>` with `type="text"`. Whatever the user has typed lives in its `.value`:

```html id=page1
<input id="username" type="text" value="Hello world">
```

```{javascript autorun}
const input = document.querySelector('#username');

console.log(input.value);

input.value = 'changed from JavaScript';

console.log(input.value);
```
```+id=page1
```

Reading `.value` gives us the text, and assigning to it changes what the field shows.

### Focus, Select, and Disable

Three more controls, all worth knowing:

- **`input.focus()`** puts the keyboard cursor in the field. Most browsers draw a colored outline around the focused element.
- **`input.select()`** highlights all the text already in the field, so that if the user starts typing it replaces the lot.
- **`input.disabled = true`** grays the field out and stops it being edited. `input.disabled = false` re-enables it.

Setting `.disabled` does the same job as the attribute methods from the last lesson: `input.disabled = true` is equivalent to `input.setAttribute('disabled', '')`, and `input.disabled = false` is equivalent to `input.removeAttribute('disabled')`. For a lot of people the property is the more straightforward of the two, since you say `true` or `false` and mean it.

### A Worked Example: Greet and Refocus

Let's build something with all of this. The user types a name and clicks a button, and we show a greeting.

```html id=page2
<label for="name">Enter your name</label>
<input id="name" type="text">
<button id="greet">Greet me</button>
<p id="greeting"></p>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const input = document.querySelector('#name');
const button = document.querySelector('#greet');
const greetingP = document.querySelector('#greeting');

function onGreet() {
  greetingP.textContent = 'Hello, ' + input.value;
}

button.addEventListener('click', onGreet);
```
```+id=page2
```

Type a name and click the button. It works, but try greeting a second person: you have to click back into the field and delete the old name first. We can fix that in two lines.

```{javascript autorun}
const input = document.querySelector('#name');
const button = document.querySelector('#greet');
const greetingP = document.querySelector('#greeting');

function onGreet() {
  greetingP.textContent = 'Hello, ' + input.value;
  input.value = '';
  input.focus();
}

button.addEventListener('click', onGreet);
```
```+id=page2
```

Now clicking the button empties the field and puts the cursor back in it, so the next name can be typed straight away.

An alternative to clearing the field is `input.select()`, which leaves the old name in place but highlights it, so typing replaces it. Which one you want depends on whether the previous value is worth keeping on screen.

### Tidying Up What the User Typed

Whatever comes out of `.value` is exactly what the user typed, spaces and capitals included, and that is rarely what you want to compare against. Two string methods do almost all of the cleaning up.

- **`.trim()`** returns the string with any spaces at the start and end removed.
- **`.toLowerCase()`** returns the string with every letter in lowercase. There is a matching `.toUpperCase()`.

Here is a value with both problems, spaces around it and a stray capital:

```html id=page3
<input id="word" type="text" value="  JavaScript  ">
```

```{javascript autorun}
const input = document.querySelector('#word');

console.log(input.value.length);
console.log(input.value.trim().length);

console.log(input.value.trim() === 'javascript');
console.log(input.value.trim().toLowerCase() === 'javascript');
```
```+id=page3
```

The raw value has stray spaces and a capital S, so comparing it directly fails. Trimming and lowercasing first makes the comparison succeed.

Note that neither method changes the input; both **return a new string**, leaving `input.value` exactly as it was. If you want the field itself tidied up, assign the result back: `input.value = input.value.trim();`

`.trim()` is also the usual way to reject an entry that is empty or only spaces:

```html id=page4
<input id="word" type="text" value="     ">
```

```{javascript autorun}
const input = document.querySelector('#word');

if (input.value.trim() === '') {
  console.log('nothing was entered');
} else {
  console.log('got: ' + input.value.trim());
}
```
```+id=page4
```

## Checkboxes and `.checked`

A checkbox is an `<input>` with `type="checkbox"`, and its on/off status lives in `.checked`, not in `.value`:

```html id=page5
<input id="subscribe" type="checkbox">
<label for="subscribe">Email me updates</label>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const box = document.querySelector('#subscribe');

console.log('starts out: ' + box.checked);

function onClick() {
  console.log('now: ' + box.checked);
}

box.addEventListener('click', onClick);
```
```+id=page5
```

Tick and untick the box a few times. Every click logs the new status: `true` when the box is on, `false` when it is off. Reading `.checked` always gives the box's status right now, so there is nothing to keep in sync yourself.

Assigning to `.checked` works too, and moves the box on screen:

```{javascript autorun}
const box = document.querySelector('#subscribe');

console.log(box.checked);

box.checked = true;

console.log(box.checked);
```
```+id=page5
```

The box above is drawn ticked as soon as the cell runs, even though nobody clicked it.

Radio buttons work the same way: each option is its own `<input type="radio">`, and `.checked` says whether that particular one is selected.

### Always Use a Label

A checkbox on its own is a small square with no meaning, so it needs text beside it. Putting that text next to the box as plain text works visually and is worse than it looks: clicking the words does nothing, and the user has to hit the small box exactly.

The better approach is a **`<label>`** element connected to the input. Give the input an `id`, and give the label a `for` attribute with the same value:

```{html autorun}
<input type="checkbox" id="a"> No label element, so clicking this text does nothing

<input type="checkbox" id="b"> <label for="b">Properly labeled, so clicking this text toggles the box</label>
```

Try clicking each piece of text. Only the second one toggles its checkbox. The connection also matters for screen readers, which is reason enough on its own.

## Finding Which Inputs Are Checked

With several checkboxes on a page, we usually want to know which ones the user selected. **CSS pseudo-classes** let us query elements that are in a particular state, and they are written with a colon. The one we want is **`:checked`**.

```html id=page6
<label><input type="checkbox" checked> A</label>
<label><input type="checkbox"> B</label>
<label><input type="checkbox" checked> C</label>
<label><input type="checkbox"> D</label>
<button id="go">Submit</button>
```

```{javascript autorun}
const button = document.querySelector('#go');

function onClick() {
  const selectedInputs = document.querySelectorAll('input:checked');
  console.log(selectedInputs.length + ' selected');
}

button.addEventListener('click', onClick);
```
```+id=page6
```

Toggle some boxes and click Submit. `input:checked` matches every input that is currently on, and nothing else. If none are checked you get an empty NodeList, with a length of `0`.

Note that this gives back the checkbox elements themselves, not their labels.

## Exercises

1. Read the text input's value into `username`.

    ```html id=page7
    <input id="user" type="text" value="Alice">
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const input = document.querySelector('#user');

    const username = /* Your code here */;

    console.log(username);
    ```
    ```+id=page7
    ```
    ```+solution
    const input = document.querySelector('#user');

    const username = input.value;

    console.log(username);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(username).should('equal', 'Alice').run('Read the text from the input with `.value`', 'Read the input value');
    ```

2. The checkbox starts unchecked. Check it from JavaScript, and disable it so the user cannot change it back.

    ```{javascript}
    const box = document.querySelector('#subscribe');

    // Write your code here
    ```
    ```+id=page5
    ```
    ```+solution
    const box = document.querySelector('#subscribe');

    box.checked = true;
    box.disabled = true;
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(box.checked).should('equal', true).run('Set the checkbox\'s `.checked` property to `true`', 'Checked the box');
    wrap(box.disabled).should('equal', true).run('Set the checkbox\'s `.disabled` property to `true`', 'Disabled the box');
    ```

3. Count how many checkboxes are currently checked and store the number in `count`.

    ```html id=page8
    <label><input type="checkbox" checked> A</label>
    <label><input type="checkbox"> B</label>
    <label><input type="checkbox" checked> C</label>
    <label><input type="checkbox" checked> D</label>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const count = /* Your code here */;

    console.log(count);
    ```
    ```+id=page8
    ```
    ```+solution
    const count = document.querySelectorAll('input:checked').length;

    console.log(count);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(count).should('equal', 3).run('`count` should be `3`. Use `document.querySelectorAll(\'input:checked\')` and read its `length`', 'Counted the checked inputs');
    wrap(source.includes(':checked')).should('equal', true).run('Use the `:checked` pseudo-class in your selector', 'Used the :checked pseudo-class');
    ```

4. Wire up the button so that clicking it puts `You typed: ` followed by the input's value into the paragraph, then clears the input and returns keyboard focus to it.

    ```html id=page9
    <input id="entry" type="text" value="hello">
    <button id="send">Send</button>
    <p id="output"></p>
    ```

    ```{javascript}
    const input = document.querySelector('#entry');
    const button = document.querySelector('#send');
    const output = document.querySelector('#output');

    // Write your code here
    ```
    ```+id=page9
    ```
    ```+solution
    const input = document.querySelector('#entry');
    const button = document.querySelector('#send');
    const output = document.querySelector('#output');

    function onSend() {
      output.textContent = 'You typed: ' + input.value;
      input.value = '';
      input.focus();
    }

    button.addEventListener('click', onSend);
    ```
    ```+test
    assert('#output').should('have.text', '').run('The paragraph should stay empty until the button is clicked', 'Nothing happens before the click');
    assert('#send').click().run('Could not find the `#send` button', 'Clicked the button');
    assert('#output').should('have.text', 'You typed: hello').run('Clicking should set the paragraph to `You typed: ` followed by the input value', 'Showed what was typed');
    wrap(input.value).should('equal', '').run('Clicking should also clear the input by setting `input.value` to an empty string', 'Cleared the input');
    wrap(source.includes('.focus()')).should('equal', true).run('Call `input.focus()` so the user can type again straight away', 'Returned focus to the input');
    ```

